{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And all the people saw the voices, etc.</b> One must investigate this matter: how did they see that which is heard? And what is the meaning of what it says afterward, ״וַיַּרְא הָעָם וַיָּנֻעוּ וַיַּעַמְדוּ מֵרָחֹק״ (Exodus 20:15), after it has already been said ״וְכָל הָעָם רֹאִים״ etc. — if so, why do I need ״וַיַּרְא״? And likewise, what is written ״דַּבֵּר אַתָּה עִמָּנוּ וְנִשְׁמָעָה״ (Exodus 20:16) — the word ״וְנִשְׁמָעָה״ is superfluous." "Now it is nearly self-evident that every single utterance that went forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, immediately took on corporeal form, and that utterance had so much tangibility that they could see in the air all the letters floating, as though everything were written before them. And a proof of this is from what is written (Psalms 33:6): ״בִּדְבַר ה׳ שָׁמַיִם נַעֲשׂוּ״ etc. — see, every utterance that goes forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, creates a new creation. And likewise, regarding the formation of the universe, they said (Hagigah 14a) that from every utterance that goes forth from the mouth of the Holy One, blessed be He, an angel is created. And so too all these Utterances — after they went forth from the mouth of the Gevurah, they had tangibility. Therefore our Sages of blessed memory said (Pesahim 87b) that when Moses broke the tablets the letters were floating — and if there had been no tangibility to the letters, how could they have floated? And a greater proof than this: concerning the latter tablets it is written (Exodus 34:1) ״וְכָתַבְתִּי עַל הַלֻּחֹת אֶת הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר הָיוּ עַל הַלֻּחֹת הָרִאשֹׁנִים״ — it does not say ״כַּדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר הָיוּ,״ but rather ״אֶת הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר הָיוּ,״ teaching that those very letters which floated from the first tablets became fixed in the second ones. If so, there certainly was tangibility in those letters. Therefore it says ״וְכָל הָעָם רֹאִים אֶת הַקּוֹלֹת״ — that is, the voice of Elohim speaking to them: those voices they saw with their eyes." "You will understand and know that the sense of sight is more refined in man than the sense of hearing, for a person can see at a distance of many mils, but cannot hear at so great a distance, because the sense of hearing does not hold sway so far as the sense of sight. And Israel were terrified on every account by the voice of Elohim, for it is awesome. Therefore, when Israel saw that they were able to see the voice of His word, blessed be He, they thought: even if we stand at a distance and do not hear the voice speaking to us, it is still enough for us if we see before us the form of the letters in the air. Hence it says ״וַיַּרְא הָעָם וַיָּנֻעוּ וַיַּעַמְדוּ מֵרָחֹק״ (Exodus 20:15) — that is to say, because ״וַיַּרְא הָעָם,״ they saw the form of the letters, therefore ״וַיַּעַמְדוּ מֵרָחֹק,״ for they said: by sight alone it is sufficient for us, even without hearing. But afterward Israel regretted it and said: why should HaShem stand at a distance? Is it not better for us to draw near to the place of His holiness? And in order to escape the dread of the voice, they said to Moses ״דַּבֵּר אַתָּה עִמָּנוּ וְנִשְׁמָעָה״ (Exodus 20:16) — for even though hearing is not equivalent to sight, it is still enough for us when we hear, even if we do not see the entire form of the letters. And our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 88b) that when Israel retreated backward, the ministering angels thrust them forward to bring them near the Shekhinah, etc. — and concerning that moment it was said, the verse ״דַּבֵּר אַתָּה עִמָּנוּ וְנִשְׁמָעָה.״ Even though Scripture did not explain that the angels were thrusting them forward, nevertheless David came and explained it, saying ״מַלְכֵי צְבָאוֹת יִדֹּדוּן יִדֹּדוּן״ etc. (Psalms 68:13)."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "The word יִדֹּדוּן in Psalms 68:13 is read here in the sense of דּוֹד ('to move/push'), consistent with the midrashic reading cited from Shabbat 88b."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:05.837978480Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}